# Line endings: LF everywhere, in the index AND in the working tree, no
# matter what the cloning machine's core.autocrlf is set to.
#
# This is not cosmetic here. The build runs on Linux/macOS (theos) — on
# Windows, inside WSL against a checkout that may live on an NTFS path — and
# several tracked files break outright, not merely look untidy, if they are
# checked out with CRLF:
#
#   * build_and_sign.sh      — a CRLF shebang line makes the kernel look for
#                              an interpreter named "/bin/bash\r"
#   * layout/DEBIAN/postinst — same, and it fails at install time on-device,
#                              where it is least convenient to debug
#   * Makefile               — theos' own included makefiles and recipe lines
#                              are whitespace-sensitive
#   * control                — dpkg control fields are parsed line-wise
#
# `text=auto` still lets git detect genuinely binary content and leave it
# untouched, so this is safe as a blanket default; `eol=lf` is the part that
# overrides a local core.autocrlf=true on checkout.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Spelled out for the extensionless and build-critical files above, so the
# intent survives anyone narrowing the glob later.
build_and_sign.sh      text eol=lf
fixtures/appa/build_and_sign.sh text eol=lf
layout/DEBIAN/postinst text eol=lf
Makefile               text eol=lf
fixtures/appa/Makefile text eol=lf
control                text eol=lf

# Sources and data consumed by `\n`-oriented parsers.
*.x      text eol=lf
*.m      text eol=lf
*.h      text eol=lf
*.c      text eol=lf
*.py     text eol=lf
*.sh     text eol=lf
*.plist  text eol=lf
*.yaml   text eol=lf
*.yml    text eol=lf
*.md     text eol=lf
*.pem    text eol=lf

# Build output and key material are gitignored, but mark the formats binary
# anyway so an accidental `git add -f` cannot mangle them.
*.deb binary
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.p12 binary
*.mobileprovision binary
